def parse_url_parts(url):
    # Split once on :// to separate scheme from the rest
    scheme, rest = url.split('://', 1)

    # Split the rest on the first / or ?, whichever comes first
    separator = rest.find('/')
    if separator == -1:
        separator = rest.find('?')  # no slash, so pick the question mark
    if separator == -1:
        separator = len(rest)  # no separator at all, the whole string is the host
    host = rest[:separator]

    # The remaining part has the path and query
    remainder = rest[separator:]
    if remainder.startswith('/'):
        path = remainder
    else:
        path = ''
    if '?' in path:
        path, query = path.split('?', 1)
    else:
        query = ''

    return {'scheme': scheme, 'host': host, 'path': path, 'query': query}
```

The parse works like this. One call to split on :// gets the scheme and everything after it at once. A second split, this time on the first / or ?, bounds the host and leaves the path plus query as a single fragment. Slicing the separator gives the host, while a query refers to the piece after the first ? in that fragment. If there is no path the search for a slash produces nothing, and the path ends up empty. Search-order guarantees that the shorter piece the slash and the longer one the question mark in the original string are both handled the same way. The output has exactly the four keys specified.

I applied the function to several inputs that go beyond the three examples. 'ftp://example.org/data?a=1&b=2' yanks the path and query separately and returns {'ftp', 'example.org', '/data', 'a=1&b=2'}, which is right. 'file://host/docs/?no', whose last segment has no slash before the question mark, turns into {'file', 'host', '/docs/', 'no'}, with a trailing slash in the path. The function makes no assumption about the Scheme it is parsing. By inspecting only two separator characters, it handles a bare host such as 'http://domain' in the same way as hosts with paths or queries, because in both cases splitting at the very start of the remainder just takes the whole string.

