On entering, a car that is not already parked is recorded with its entry time; a repeated entry is ignored. On exiting, a car that is parked pays (exit time minus entry time) multiplied by the rate and is deleted from the parked set; a car that is not parked on exit is ignored. Only cars with a recorded entry and recorded exit appear in the output. Because entry and exit times are non-negative integers, the time spent is non-negative, and the rate cannot cause negative fees. Deleting from the parked set matters for later exits (which should not leave double-charges or allow a car already departed to pay again). The stored entry time is always the earliest entry of a repeating car, since the park-and-re-enter case is ignored here and that earlier charge is what the exit is matched against.

