def rectangles_overlap(r1, r2):
    return not ((r1[2] <= r2[0]) or (r2[2] <= r1[0]) or (r1[3] <= r2[1]) or (r2[3] <= r1[1]))
```

The formula is the standard exclusion condition from computational geometry: two axis-aligned rectangles whose interiors do not touch fall into one of four categories, each captured by one clause. The clause r1[2] <= r2[0] holds when the right side of the first is at the left of the second; r2[2] <= r1[0] holds when the right of the second is at the left of the first; the other two clauses are for vertical separation on the top/bottom sides. If none holds, an interior intersection must exist, and the function returns True, otherwise False.

It is shorter to write the actual overlap condition, requiring the rectangles to share both a horizontal and a vertical span:

    return (r1[2] > r2[0] and r2[2] > r1[0]) and (r1[3] > r2[1] and r2[3] > r1[1])

The result is the same either way.

The three example rectangles all have vertices at integer coordinates, and none of them involves direction because the coordinates are already sorted by the input spec, so I worked with a setup that is not represented: r1 = (0.1, 0.1, 1.0, 1.0) and r2 = (0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1.4). The right edge of r1 lines up with the left edge of r2, which is a touching edge rather than an overlapping region, and r1's right edge is inside those of r2 on the bottom and top. The overlap function returns False, which is correct.

