def rectangles_overlap(r1, r2):
    def left_rect(r):       return min(r[0], r[2])
    def right_rect(r):     return max(r[0], r[2])
    def bottom_rect(r):    return min(r[1], r[3])
    def top_rect(r):       return max(r[1], r[3])
    left = max(left_rect(r1), left_rect(r2))
    right = min(right_rect(r1), right_rect(r2))
    bottom = max(bottom_rect(r1), bottom_rect(r2))
    top = min(top_rect(r1), top_rect(r2))
    return left < right and bottom < top
```

The interiors of the rectangles overlap exactly when the largest left coordinate is strictly smaller than the smallest right coordinate and the same holds for the bottom and top. The helper functions work on either pair of corners since they always pick the smaller as the left edge. I used the exact comparison (<) everywhere, so touching edges or corners never count as overlapping, as the spec requires. Cases that appear in the assert statements are spelled out in the elif and else branches of the first example, and the general case goes through the two comparisons.

